WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.421
Picture a disability services call line on a Monday morning.

00:00:03.421 --> 00:00:06.843
Calls about a new authorization letter have doubled. Wait times

00:00:06.843 --> 00:00:10.264
are up. Two staff members have given callers different answers,

00:00:10.264 --> 00:00:13.685
and a county partner has noticed. Your manager asks a

00:00:13.685 --> 00:00:16.080
simple question. Do you need more people?

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.957
Most supervisors would say yes, and some would be right.

00:00:19.957 --> 00:00:23.485
But consider what research on decisions has found. Paul Nutt

00:00:23.485 --> 00:00:27.012
studied 356 real decisions in organizations across the United States

00:00:27.012 --> 00:00:30.539
and Canada. About a quarter were made in public agencies.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:34.066
Managers considered more than one option in fewer than one

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:37.594
in five of those decisions. When they did, success rose

00:00:37.594 --> 00:00:39.710
from 56 percent to 70 percent.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.110
The most common way to fail was to settle on

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.940
a solution early and stop looking.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:48.401
There is a second trap, and it is less obvious.

00:00:48.401 --> 00:00:51.512
You might think the answer is careful problem-finding: what went

00:00:51.512 --> 00:00:54.623
wrong, and who did it. Nutt found that this approach

00:00:54.623 --> 00:00:57.734
worked no better than skipping diagnosis altogether. Problems invite blame.

00:00:57.734 --> 00:01:00.846
When people feel blamed, they defend themselves instead of helping

00:01:00.846 --> 00:01:03.957
you find the answer. So start somewhere else. Name the

00:01:03.957 --> 00:01:04.890
result you want.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:08.162
Callers should get a correct answer on the first call.

00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:11.083
Now the question is how to close a gap, and

00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:11.960
everyone can help.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.706
Next, look at real cases. Pull ten recent calls. What

00:01:15.706 --> 00:01:19.102
did people actually ask? In this example, seven callers read

00:01:19.102 --> 00:01:22.498
the same paragraph of the letter aloud. Three calls were

00:01:22.498 --> 00:01:25.894
answered from an older script that no longer matches policy.

00:01:25.894 --> 00:01:28.950
That is two causes, not one, and both matter.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.660
Notice something else. Your staff had probably known about that

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.020
paragraph for weeks. In a study of hospital nurses, researchers

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.380
watched them work around problems ninety-three percent of the time.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.740
The nurses kept patients cared for and moved on. They

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.100
rarely told anyone who could fix the cause. That is

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.460
not carelessness. It is commitment. But it keeps problems invisible.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.010
When a staff member tells you about a pattern, thank

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.610
them, and act on it.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.323
Be careful, too, about the person nearest the error. Retraining

00:01:58.323 --> 00:02:01.686
the two specialists who gave conflicting answers feels decisive. Studies

00:02:01.686 --> 00:02:05.049
of improvement efforts show that managers tend to blame workers

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:08.412
rather than the system. A quick dip in errors can

00:02:08.412 --> 00:02:11.775
make that seem true. Meanwhile, the old script is still

00:02:11.775 --> 00:02:13.120
on the shared drive.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.670
Finally, choose fixes that change the process. A clearer letter.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.870
A single current script. A review of health care investigations

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:23.070
found that teams often settle for reminders and posters, and

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.270
the same error returns. So bring your cases to the

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.470
person or area responsible for the letter and the script.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.670
Keep an interim answer in place for today's callers. And

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.870
set a date to check whether the calls actually drop.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.711
That last step is how you know the diagnosis was

00:02:39.711 --> 00:02:40.060
right.
